<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Léopold Sédar Senghor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1906">1906</a>–<a href="/years/2001">2001</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Leopold Sedar Senghor</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Léopold Sédar Senghor was a Senegalese poet, philosopher and statesman, the first president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> from 1960 to 1980. A founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/negritude">Négritude</a> movement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aime-cesaire">Aimé Césaire</a>, he sat in the French National Assembly from 1945 and led Senegal to independence, briefly within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-federation">Mali Federation</a>. In 1983 he became the first African elected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/academie-francaise">Académie française</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leopold-sedar-senghor.jpg" alt="Léopold Sédar Senghor" width="320" height="436" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dominique Roger · CC BY-SA 3.0 igo · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:UNESCO_History,_Visite_de_S._Exc._M._L%C3%A9opold_Sedar_Senghor,_Pr%C3%A9sident_de_la_R%C3%A9publique_du_S%C3%A9n%C3%A9gal_-_UNESCO_-_PHOTO0000002688_0001_(cropped).tiff" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
